Docker ENTRYPOINT vs CMD — finally made simple 🚀

🔹 ENTRYPOINT
Defines the main application that always runs when the container starts. It turns your container into an executable.

🔹 CMD
Provides default arguments to ENTRYPOINT (or a default command if ENTRYPOINT isn’t set). It can be overridden.

💡 In short:
ENTRYPOINT = WHAT runs
CMD = WITH WHAT arguments

📌 Example:

ENTRYPOINT ["python", "app.py"]
CMD ["--port", "8080"]

Run normally:
docker run myapp
➡ Runs: python app.py --port 8080

Override CMD:
docker run myapp --port 9090
➡ Runs: python app.py --port 9090

ENTRYPOINT stays. CMD changes.

Understanding this helps in: 
✔️ Writing flexible containers
✔️ Creating reusable images
✔️ Running apps correctly in Kubernetes


often asked question: 

1. If both are present in your dockerfile then what will happen?
2. If two CMD are present in your dockerfile then what will happen?


If both ENTRYPOINT and CMD are present in a Dockerfile
Docker combines them.
👉 ENTRYPOINT = main command
👉 CMD = default arguments to ENTRYPOINT

If two CMD instructions exist in a Dockerfile, Only the last CMD is used.
Docker ignores previous CMDs.



----
Below is the more simplified

1. If both ENTRYPOINT and CMD are present?
If both are defined, they work together in a parent-child relationship.

ENTRYPOINT becomes the fixed executable for the container.
CMD becomes the default arguments passed to that executable.

How it works:

If your Dockerfile looks like this:

Dockerfile
ENTRYPOINT ["python3", "app.py"] CMD ["--port", "8080"] 
When you run the container normally, it executes: python3 app.py --port 8080

The "Override" catch: If a user runs your container with an extra argument (e.g., docker run my-image --port 9090), the CMD is completely ignored and replaced by the user's input. The ENTRYPOINT remains fixed.


2. If two CMD instructions are present?

Docker is quite strict here: Only the last CMD instruction will take effect.
If your Dockerfile looks like this:

Dockerfile
CMD ["echo", "Hello World"] 
CMD ["echo", "Goodbye World"] 

Docker will completely ignore the first line. When the container starts, it will only output: Goodbye World.


